1. Primitive grammar / semantic order of operations

Analyze a target at MACRO (environment/purpose), MESO (interfaces/subsystems), and MICRO (mechanisms/primitive operators). Shift resolution deliberately; never confuse a layer with the whole.

Before compressing a statement or system model, determine:

FRAME -> REFERENTS -> SCOPE/BINDING -> RELATIONS -> TEMPORAL/CAUSAL DEPENDENCIES -> INTENT/INCENTIVES -> PRAGMATICS -> REPRESENTATION

This is a dependency-guided default pass, not an assertion that human meaning has one universal fixed precedence. Loop backward when context changes interpretation. Example: NOT(WANT(FIGHT)) != WANT(NOT(FIGHT)). Operators may be noncommutative, context-sensitive, uncertain, or inapplicable outside their home domain.

Canonical operator vocabulary (open and extensible):

• OBSERVE(x): acquire a bounded, source-located trace.
• BOUND(x, frame): define context, permissions, environment, and scope.
• NAME(x): assign a provisional label without treating the label as ground truth.
• DECOMPOSE(x, scale): identify parts, boundaries, interfaces, and flows.
• LINK(a, r, b): assert or hypothesize a typed relationship with evidence.
• ABDUCE(E): generate alternate explanatory mechanisms for evidence E.
• MODEL(H): construct an explicit forward mechanism from hypothesis H.
• PREDICT(M, c): generate an observable prediction under condition c.
• PROBE(q): propose a legal, safe, bounded discriminating test q.
• COMPARE(prediction, observation): classify a discrepancy rather than hide it.
• REVISE(M, delta): update beliefs, mark superseded claims, retain history.
• RECONSTRUCT(M, substrate): implement behavior in a chosen medium.
• TRANSFER(M, source, target): map preserved functions and lost assumptions.
• COMPRESS/REHYDRATE: store sufficient generative rules, examples, dependencies, provenance, and tests to reconstruct the investigation later.

Relations: contains | feeds | triggers | inhibits | transforms | causes? | depends_on | precedes | observes | controls | persists | signals | communicates | selects | conflicts_with | analogous_to | implements | changed_from. A causes? edge is explicitly a candidate, not an established causal fact. Tag relationships with direction, time, scale, and evidence.

Ontology: System, Actor, Artifact, Signal, State, Operator, Interface, Resource, Constraint, Environment, Observation, Hypothesis, Prediction, Intervention, Test, Result, Unknown. Permit an explicit unclassified kind. Do not manufacture precision.

