2. The eight-pass ARKFORGE investigation

P0 — Charter and boundary

Record the target, intended question, desired reuse, operating context, permissible observations/actions, relevant rights/consents, time horizon, risks, and stopping budget. Prefer passive analysis of authorized materials. Do not run unfamiliar executables unsandboxed; do not bypass access controls or gather private data without authorization.

P1 — Acquire and ledger

Collect available traces: documents, images, code, measured behavior, interviews (with consent), histories, logs, public records, experimental results. For each: record original source, date/version, method/tool, location, conditions, limits, and artifact hash if applicable. Use read-only methods initially. Keep raw material distinct from interpretation.

P2 — Structural parse

Build a typed, multi-layer directed graph, with entities as nodes and evidence-linked relations as edges. Identify boundaries, feedback, inputs/outputs, control variables, state transitions, and what is missing. Apply Semantic Order of Operations before Semantic PCA/compression. Recover an operator graph, not a mere list of nouns.

P3 — Abductive reconstruction

For each unexplained observation, generate at least two plausible mechanisms when the available evidence does not uniquely resolve it. Distinguish:

• Observed: directly available as a trace under known conditions.
• Derived: result of a transparent transformation of established traces.
• Inferred: defensible but not directly established.
• Hypothesized: candidate explanation awaiting a test.
• Unknown / Unavailable: not established / could not be examined.

Record what each model explains, what it fails to explain, what would falsify it, and the assumptions it imports. Prefer explanatory compression but penalize unsupported complexity. Do not equate a tidy story with truth.

P4 — Discriminating probes

Choose the smallest feasible experiment or observation that separates candidate models. Seek counterexamples, boundary conditions, negative controls, and independent measurement. Rank probes by expected discriminatory information, feasibility, risk, cost, and reversibility. If controlled interventions are unsafe, unethical, or impossible, use observational predictions and lower confidence accordingly.

P5 — Forward instantiation

Construct the smallest functional replica, process model, equation, executable, schematic, story grammar, or other appropriate generator. Define inputs, state, transition rules, outputs, limits, and expected traces. Keep original structure separate from new adaptation. A functioning simulator is not assumed unless actually implemented and run.

P6 — Delta Calculus

Run safe/authorized tests where possible; otherwise report tests as proposed. For each difference delta = observation - prediction (or a typed semantic mismatch), classify:
measurement_noise | mistaken_reference | wrong_scope | missing_variable | wrong_operator | wrong_order | changed_boundary | regime_change | model_overfit | adversarial_or_manipulated_signal | unresolved.

Pinpoint where the explanatory chain first fails. Compare at the same scale and conditions. Revise the model, retain the prior revision and its falsifier, and recurse; do not silently rewrite history.

P7 — Rebuild, transfer, export

Deliver: a falsifiable operator model; usable reconstruction appropriate to the domain; tests/limitations; evidence-linked residual unknowns; and optionally a transfer into a new substrate. Where no safe test or source exists, deliver an investigation plan, not a claim of reconstruction. Export a compact semantic seed plus enough provenance to audit and rehydrate.

