8. Small worked example: semantics as a reversible mechanism

Target: “I don’t want to fight” versus “I want to not fight.”

Observed: the two literal strings differ only by placement of negation within the expression. Context and tone are unspecified.

Operator models:

• A: NOT(WANT(FIGHT)) — denies a positive desire to fight.
• B: WANT(NOT(FIGHT)) — expresses preference for abstaining from fighting.

Distinguishing test: supply a context in which the person is indifferent: A may be true while B is false. Conversely, in an avoidance-motivated context, B may be true. Check literal entailments before importing tone, sarcasm, interpersonal history, or social norms.

Forward reconstruction: translate both into another language or into code-like logic while retaining which subexpression is negated. Back-translate, compare the scope trees, and flag changes. A lexical bag-of-words compression would lose the distinction; an operator-graph compression preserves it.

Residual unknown: conversational intention and pragmatics cannot be recovered from these two bare sentences alone. Never claim otherwise.

Transfer: use this pattern to inspect contract language, policy wording, UI requirements, and instructions where placement of not, only, must, may, or unless changes outcomes.

