9. Quality gates / acceptance tests

Before marking a dossier validated, check:

☐ Scope, permission, and intended use declared.
☐ Raw observations not silently converted into mechanisms.
☐ Every material inference has evidence or an explicit assumption.
☐ Hidden variables, scope, and order-of-operations considered.
☐ At least one alternative explanation tested when ambiguity matters.
☐ Forward model generated genuinely new, checkable predictions.
☐ Proposed tests not misreported as performed.
☐ A negative or adversarial case and a boundary condition considered.
☐ Out-of-sample checks used where data permits.
☐ Residuals and coverage/limitations documented.
☐ Reconstruction evaluated for stated conditions only.
☐ Transferred function tested independently in the target substrate.
☐ Provenance and original observations retained; sensitive data redacted.
☐ Another agent can reconstruct the investigation from the seed without inventing facts.

Gold-standard regression fixtures for future runtime:

1. Semantic scope: NOT(WANT(X)) must not normalize to WANT(NOT(X)).
2. Confounded process: correlated events must not yield an unconditional causal edge.
3. Software boundary: static imported function must not be labeled runtime-executed without execution evidence.
4. Partial coverage: missing logs must not be treated as confirmation that a behavior never happens.
5. Source mutation: changed source digest must trigger a new evidence commitment.
6. Alternative models: identical observations under two mechanisms must retain both until a probe discriminates.
7. Transfer loss: analogy between living and digital systems must flag important missing substrate assumptions.
8. Rehydration: a fresh model can reproduce target, alternatives, limits, and next test from this one file.

Definition of done: a dossier is documented when its claims and uncertainty are tracked; modeled when a forward mechanism exists; tested when a specified probe has actually run; validated within scope only when held-out predictions match at declared tolerances. Never collapse these states into one label.

